rubber-motor-answer-why =
    Each turn winds another piece of band onto the pencil. Twice the turns — the band is stretched twice as far, so it pulls twice as hard (Hooke’s law). And it pulls until it unwinds — over a way twice as long too. Twice as hard and twice as long — four times as far; three times the turns — nine times. A real band gives back a little less than the model, but the rule shows all the same.

    The rubber motor is one of the oldest. In 1871 the Frenchman Alphonse Pénaud flew a model aeroplane driven by a twisted rubber band — the “Planophore” — in the Tuileries Gardens in Paris: it went 40 m in 11 seconds. And in 1878 their father brought his sons, Wilbur and Orville Wright, a toy helicopter after Pénaud’s invention — of paper, bamboo and cork, its rotor turned by a rubber band. The brothers played with it until it broke, then built their own. Later they said that toy was where their passion for flying began.
